1
00:00:03,377 --> 00:00:05,338
- Hey.
- Morning.

2
00:00:06,881 --> 00:00:08,716
- Coffee?
- Thank you.

3
00:00:11,802 --> 00:00:13,888
- Made you breakfast.
- Oh, you shouldn't have.

4
00:00:14,013 --> 00:00:15,806
Well, when there's a guest,
I like to make a fuss.

5
00:00:15,932 --> 00:00:18,434
- And I like the fuss you make.
- Yeah?

6
00:00:25,316 --> 00:00:28,027
- Something wrong?
- Just... Work stuff.

7
00:00:28,694 --> 00:00:31,280
Listen, I was wondering
if we could get dinner tonight.

8
00:00:31,405 --> 00:00:34,116
Or... Is that weird because
we went out last night?

9
00:00:34,242 --> 00:00:38,371
- Is... is two nights in a row weird?
- No. I don't think it's weird.

10
00:00:38,496 --> 00:00:40,498
Okay, good.
I'll make a reservation.

11
00:00:40,748 --> 00:00:43,834
- A reservation, huh?
- Like I said, I like to make a fuss.

12
00:00:45,419 --> 00:00:47,213
I have to go to work.

13
00:00:49,048 --> 00:00:50,675
I will take this to go.

14
00:00:55,638 --> 00:00:57,807
- I'll see you later?
- Yeah.

15
00:01:04,730 --> 00:01:06,482
- Morning, Lisbon.
- Hey, Jane.

16
00:01:06,732 --> 00:01:09,193
Hey. We just got a call
from the forest service.

17
00:01:09,318 --> 00:01:11,279
They caught a body
in Sam Houston national forest.

18
00:01:11,404 --> 00:01:12,905
Abbott wants you guys
to go check it out.

19
00:01:13,155 --> 00:01:16,409
- How far away is that?
- 100, 120 miles.

20
00:01:16,826 --> 00:01:18,953
- Is there a problem?
- No. No.

21
00:01:19,078 --> 00:01:21,539
She has plans for tonight.
She's worried about getting back in time.

22
00:01:21,664 --> 00:01:23,708
- Really?
- Actually, it is not a problem.

23
00:01:23,833 --> 00:01:27,128
Nonsense. Doesn't the FBI have helicopters?
Can't we just take one of those?

24
00:01:27,253 --> 00:01:30,214
- I guess, if it's available.
- That will not be necessary.

25
00:01:30,339 --> 00:01:31,716
Nonsense. We'll be up and back in a jiffy.

26
00:01:31,841 --> 00:01:33,926
Just tell your agent Pike
he owes me a favor.

27
00:01:54,488 --> 00:01:55,990
Hello, there!

28
00:01:56,115 --> 00:01:59,452
Officer Green! Forestry service!
Thank you so much for coming!

29
00:01:59,869 --> 00:02:00,911
This way!

30
00:02:06,709 --> 00:02:09,462
Two hikers found her
around 9:00 this morning.

31
00:02:09,712 --> 00:02:12,256
- Any identification?
- Nah. She's a Jane Doe.

32
00:02:12,381 --> 00:02:15,509
But we did find this cellphone
about 10 feet from her body.

33
00:02:15,635 --> 00:02:17,928
Must have fallen out of her pocket.
It's pretty beat-up.

34
00:02:18,679 --> 00:02:20,348
Any idea how she died?

35
00:02:20,473 --> 00:02:22,767
Well, medical examiner says
it must be a broken neck

36
00:02:22,892 --> 00:02:24,727
somewhere between
6:00 and 8:00 this morning.

37
00:02:25,061 --> 00:02:27,521
My first thought was maybe she was
hiking on the road above and fell

38
00:02:27,647 --> 00:02:29,398
but... I don't know.

39
00:02:29,523 --> 00:02:31,192
Something just seems off
about the body.

40
00:02:32,151 --> 00:02:33,611
That's why I gave you guys a call.

41
00:02:33,903 --> 00:02:37,323
- Sure hope I haven't wasted your time.
- Not at all, ranger Green.

42
00:02:37,448 --> 00:02:38,658
In fact, you're very astute.

43
00:02:39,367 --> 00:02:40,493
She wasn't hiking.

44
00:02:40,618 --> 00:02:42,328
You know, you go hiking,
you wear some socks.

45
00:02:42,453 --> 00:02:45,623
No socks...
and no blisters, either

46
00:02:45,748 --> 00:02:47,541
so I don't think
she walked here.

47
00:02:47,667 --> 00:02:50,169
New, off-brand sneakers and jeans.

48
00:02:50,419 --> 00:02:51,921
Full face of makeup.

49
00:02:53,005 --> 00:02:55,675
Expensive ginger-and-citrus shampoo.

50
00:02:57,426 --> 00:02:59,303
She's a girl of slender means

51
00:02:59,428 --> 00:03:02,098
going out to meet someone
she wanted to look good for.

52
00:03:02,223 --> 00:03:04,100
Any idea where she could have
been brought from?

53
00:03:04,225 --> 00:03:06,519
There's nothing around here
for miles, except the club.

54
00:03:06,686 --> 00:03:07,770
- Club?
- Yeah

55
00:03:07,895 --> 00:03:09,647
the Foragers' club.
It's just up the road.

56
00:03:09,772 --> 00:03:12,733
It's kind of a ritzy place.
Very exclusive... men only.

57
00:03:12,858 --> 00:03:14,110
But, nah, she didn't come from there.

58
00:03:14,235 --> 00:03:16,570
- Now, how do you know that?
- Well, I called as soon as we found her.

59
00:03:16,821 --> 00:03:18,864
All their female employees
are accounted for.

60
00:03:18,989 --> 00:03:21,158
And, like I said,
they don't allow female guests.

61
00:03:21,283 --> 00:03:22,326
No one's missing.

62
00:03:33,796 --> 00:03:36,966
"Morning. Welcome to the Foragers' club.
How may I help you?"

63
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:41,053
- We're with the FBI. Let us in, please.
- "What's this about?"

64
00:03:41,178 --> 00:03:43,514
One of your members
murdered somebody.

65
00:03:44,598 --> 00:03:46,308
"We'd like to talk to someone
about that."

66
00:03:46,434 --> 00:03:49,478
- I need to talk to someone at...
- "Hello?"

67
00:04:02,539 --> 00:04:05,000
Mr. Jane. Agent Lisbon.

68
00:04:05,709 --> 00:04:07,169
Welcome to the Foragers'.

69
00:04:07,294 --> 00:04:09,379
I'm Kenyon Russell.
I'm the president.

70
00:04:09,671 --> 00:04:12,674
Listen, before we begin
I need to ask you to wear one of these

71
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:14,510
while you're on the grounds.

72
00:04:14,635 --> 00:04:18,472
All the staff and guests wear them.
Just, one of those rules.

73
00:04:18,722 --> 00:04:20,015
- All men, huh?
- Yeah.

74
00:04:20,140 --> 00:04:23,519
{\an8}- What's that about?
- Well, it's just simple brotherhood, ma'am.

75
00:04:23,644 --> 00:04:25,854
{\an8}We're not anti-female...
not at all.

76
00:04:26,355 --> 00:04:28,899
{\an8}We're fully chartered
by the state of Texas

77
00:04:29,024 --> 00:04:31,985
{\an8}as a private,
single-sex organization.

78
00:04:32,110 --> 00:04:35,113
We have, uh, female employees...
mainly in the dining hall.

79
00:04:35,239 --> 00:04:38,575
{\an8}But, uh, we just don't accept
women for membership.

80
00:04:40,077 --> 00:04:43,497
{\an8}- Are women allowed as guests?
- Generally no

81
00:04:44,039 --> 00:04:47,626
{\an8}though there have been exceptions...
First ladies, for instance.

82
00:04:47,918 --> 00:04:50,754
{\an8}But none recently.
Please... sit.

83
00:04:51,713 --> 00:04:56,051
{\an8}I know this all sounds very old-school
but you have to understand...

84
00:04:56,176 --> 00:05:03,100
{\an8}Our members are senators, judges, C.E.O.s
They're successful, driven men.

85
00:05:03,225 --> 00:05:07,437
{\an8}They really have very few places
that they can completely relax.

86
00:05:07,563 --> 00:05:12,609
{\an8}Here, we have no cellphones, no twitter
no distractions from the outside world.

87
00:05:12,734 --> 00:05:16,154
{\an8}- Women are a distraction?
- Well, no, of course not.

88
00:05:16,280 --> 00:05:17,698
{\an8}But we offer, um, um...

89
00:05:18,740 --> 00:05:21,493
very... unique kind of camaraderie.

90
00:05:21,618 --> 00:05:25,122
{\an8}The Foragers' is a...
It's a very special place for a lot of us

91
00:05:25,247 --> 00:05:28,500
{\an8}a place our fathers and grandfathers built
and brought us to.

92
00:05:28,625 --> 00:05:30,711
{\an8}Then how did our Jane Doe
get on the property?

93
00:05:30,836 --> 00:05:32,296
{\an8}She wasn't on our property.

94
00:05:32,421 --> 00:05:35,716
{\an8}If she was, I'd know. We have excellent
security systems in place.

95
00:05:37,551 --> 00:05:38,760
Do you know this woman?

96
00:05:40,345 --> 00:05:42,890
{\an8}This is the poor woman
who died, I assume.

97
00:05:43,223 --> 00:05:44,641
No, I don't recognize her.

98
00:05:44,766 --> 00:05:47,227
{\an8}Oh, that's kind of weird,
'cause she was killed here.

99
00:05:47,352 --> 00:05:49,646
{\an8}And if she were here,
then you would know, right?

100
00:05:50,439 --> 00:05:53,400
{\an8}That's a very serious
accusation, Mr. Jane.

101
00:05:53,525 --> 00:05:54,776
- I hope you...
- The victim was found

102
00:05:54,902 --> 00:06:01,074
{\an8}with two holes in her shirt right here...
Made from a pin like this.

103
00:06:01,408 --> 00:06:05,037
{\an8}So she was wearing
one of these badges...

104
00:06:05,579 --> 00:06:06,622
Like this.

105
00:06:06,997 --> 00:06:09,666
{\an8}She was killed here, and she
was dumped outside the gate.

106
00:06:10,375 --> 00:06:14,504
{\an8}Holes in her shirt? That's a pretty,
tenuous theory, at best.

107
00:06:14,630 --> 00:06:17,674
{\an8}Tenuous?
We are 50 miles from nowhere

108
00:06:17,799 --> 00:06:20,552
{\an8}and that poor woman is dead
outside your gate.

109
00:06:20,677 --> 00:06:22,471
Tenuous, it is not, sir.

110
00:06:22,596 --> 00:06:24,973
Now, I hope we can rely
on your full cooperation.

111
00:06:27,017 --> 00:06:28,143
Yes, ma'am.

112
00:06:28,268 --> 00:06:30,020
{\an8}I can't promise
she won't hurt anyone.

113
00:06:30,145 --> 00:06:32,439
{\an8}I'll try to restrain her,
but I can't promise.

114
00:06:33,398 --> 00:06:34,441
Sorry.

115
00:06:36,985 --> 00:06:38,445
How many people
are on the grounds?

116
00:06:38,612 --> 00:06:41,698
{\an8}182 campers and 97 staff.

117
00:06:41,823 --> 00:06:43,784
{\an8}There'll be more people
coming for the talk tomorrow.

118
00:06:43,909 --> 00:06:45,160
{\an8}- The talk?
- Yeah

119
00:06:45,285 --> 00:06:47,329
we have a speaker
coming in from Silicon Valley

120
00:06:47,454 --> 00:06:50,207
to speak about digital technology
and the economy.

121
00:06:50,958 --> 00:06:53,418
We're gonna need the names of everybody
who was here last night.

122
00:06:53,543 --> 00:06:55,712
Well, our membership list
is confidential. You...

123
00:06:55,837 --> 00:07:00,008
- I can get a subpoena, if I have to.
- That won't be necessary.

124
00:07:00,133 --> 00:07:04,221
I was merely gonna counsel you
to... exercise discretion here

125
00:07:04,346 --> 00:07:08,600
for your own sake.
Our members are... Powerful people.

126
00:07:09,476 --> 00:07:11,436
You can see our concierge,
Peter Kilgallen

127
00:07:11,561 --> 00:07:12,813
and he'll give you
the information you need.

128
00:07:12,938 --> 00:07:14,648
His office is right down there
and to the right.

129
00:07:14,773 --> 00:07:16,400
- Thank you.
- Give it back!

130
00:07:16,858 --> 00:07:19,069
- Hey! Hey, give that b...
- Mr. Russell...

131
00:07:19,194 --> 00:07:21,113
- Don't treat me like a child!
- Mr. Russell.

132
00:07:21,238 --> 00:07:24,700
I'm sorry. I found Mr. Gryska
using his cellphone.

133
00:07:24,825 --> 00:07:26,076
Marvin.

134
00:07:26,201 --> 00:07:27,995
- What are you gonna do? Spank me?
- You know the rule.

135
00:07:28,120 --> 00:07:30,998
- There's no cellphones on the grounds.
- I was talking to my lawyer.

136
00:07:31,123 --> 00:07:33,041
- I have matters that need attention.
- Okay, all right, Marvin

137
00:07:33,166 --> 00:07:36,503
if you have business to do you're welcome
to take care of it off the grounds, please.

138
00:07:36,628 --> 00:07:41,216
- Oh, forget it. Who are you?
- Teresa Lisbon, FBI.

139
00:07:41,341 --> 00:07:42,926
Oh, here about
the dead girl, huh?

140
00:07:43,427 --> 00:07:46,471
That's great. First woman here in
a hundred years... she's a fed.

141
00:07:47,848 --> 00:07:49,725
- Excuse me.
- Charmer.

142
00:07:50,267 --> 00:07:54,563
Marvin Gryska is one of our,
newer members.

143
00:07:55,105 --> 00:07:56,690
I'll let Kilgallen know
you're coming.

144
00:07:56,815 --> 00:07:58,984
- Good luck with the investigation.
- Thank you.

145
00:07:59,985 --> 00:08:02,404
I think you can deal
with Kilgallen.

146
00:08:03,488 --> 00:08:05,866
- I'm gonna take a walk.
- What for?

147
00:08:07,576 --> 00:08:10,412
- Men stuff.
- Men stuff? Okay.

148
00:08:11,121 --> 00:08:12,998
Any luck identifying
our forest victim?

149
00:08:13,123 --> 00:08:14,458
We didn't get any matches
off fingerprints.

150
00:08:14,583 --> 00:08:16,877
Facial recognition
didn't pull up any names, either.

151
00:08:17,169 --> 00:08:18,795
What about the cellphone they found?

152
00:08:19,546 --> 00:08:21,673
Techs are still working on it
but haven't dug anything out yet.

153
00:08:21,798 --> 00:08:24,217
Hey. Spoke to the manager
of a motel in the area.

154
00:08:24,342 --> 00:08:26,178
He said a woman matching
our victim's description

155
00:08:26,303 --> 00:08:27,429
checked in yesterday morning

156
00:08:27,554 --> 00:08:29,473
but according to housekeeping,
she hadn't slept in her room.

157
00:08:29,931 --> 00:08:31,892
He faxed me a copy
of her driver's license.

158
00:08:32,642 --> 00:08:33,685
That's her.

159
00:08:35,020 --> 00:08:36,897
Madison Pryce. Austin address.

160
00:08:37,022 --> 00:08:38,607
I tried calling her apartment.
Nobody answered.

161
00:08:38,732 --> 00:08:39,858
I spoke to the super
in the building.

162
00:08:39,983 --> 00:08:42,444
He said Pryce's younger sister's
been staying with her.

163
00:08:42,736 --> 00:08:45,572
- I'll bring her in.
- All right. Let Lisbon and Jane know.

164
00:08:49,785 --> 00:08:52,829
- Mr. Kilgallen?
- Oh, you must be agent Lisbon, right?

165
00:08:52,954 --> 00:08:54,664
- Yeah.
- Yeah. Here you go.

166
00:08:54,790 --> 00:08:56,625
The names and contact numbers
of all the staff.

167
00:08:56,750 --> 00:08:59,002
Also, all the members
that were here last night as of 7:00.

168
00:08:59,127 --> 00:09:00,629
That's when we close the gates.

169
00:09:00,754 --> 00:09:02,881
Keep that to yourself, though.
It's highly confidential.

170
00:09:03,006 --> 00:09:04,049
I heard.

171
00:09:04,591 --> 00:09:07,010
Does the name Madison Pryce
mean anything to you?

172
00:09:07,552 --> 00:09:10,514
Madison Pryce... No. No.

173
00:09:10,639 --> 00:09:12,641
Everyone heard about the woman
who died, though. Is that her name?

174
00:09:12,891 --> 00:09:15,977
- You're certain she wasn't on staff here?
- Oh, yeah. Absolutely.

175
00:09:16,103 --> 00:09:17,813
No one by that name's worked here
since I started

176
00:09:17,938 --> 00:09:20,148
and that's about...
that's about five years now.

177
00:09:20,398 --> 00:09:23,693
- What do you do exactly?
- Provide things for the campers...

178
00:09:23,819 --> 00:09:27,280
Anything from a 120-year-old bottle
of brandy to a snakebite kit.

179
00:09:27,405 --> 00:09:29,491
You wouldn't believe some of the stuff
I've had to track down.

180
00:09:29,616 --> 00:09:30,951
I'll bet.

181
00:09:32,911 --> 00:09:35,163
Listen, I don't mean to tell you
your business.

182
00:09:35,288 --> 00:09:38,125
But I think you're barking up
the wrong tree.

183
00:09:38,416 --> 00:09:40,585
- That woman... Madison Pryce, was it?
- Yeah.

184
00:09:40,710 --> 00:09:45,048
- Yeah. She has nothing to do with us.
- Your boss, Russell, said the same thing.

185
00:09:46,633 --> 00:09:47,884
Maybe we're right, then.

186
00:09:53,265 --> 00:09:54,808
Alan. Alan!

187
00:09:55,225 --> 00:09:58,019
Stop telling me to be realistic.

188
00:09:58,520 --> 00:10:01,439
Just tell me how this ends well
for me, 'cause I...

189
00:10:02,023 --> 00:10:03,150
I got to go.

190
00:10:04,234 --> 00:10:06,736
- I won't narc. Don't worry.
- Thanks.

191
00:10:06,862 --> 00:10:08,655
Use your phone, they make you
assume the position.

192
00:10:10,157 --> 00:10:12,576
You were with that woman.
You're FBI.

193
00:10:12,868 --> 00:10:14,911
- "With," not "of."
- Yeah, well, back up, g-man.

194
00:10:15,036 --> 00:10:18,373
- I got nothing to say to you vultures.
- You've met the feds before now, I take it.

195
00:10:18,498 --> 00:10:20,667
- Oh, you don't read the financial pages?
- Life's too short.

196
00:10:20,792 --> 00:10:22,169
Your guys are
throwing the book at me.

197
00:10:22,294 --> 00:10:25,255
Conspiracy to commit
securities fraud, wire fraud

198
00:10:25,380 --> 00:10:27,674
bank fraud,
false S.E.C. filing.

199
00:10:28,508 --> 00:10:30,635
And your lawyer's trying to convince you
to make a deal.

200
00:10:30,760 --> 00:10:32,679
- Yeah... the schmuck.
- Will you?

201
00:10:33,972 --> 00:10:35,223
- The hell I will.
- Bravo.

202
00:10:35,348 --> 00:10:37,309
Quick question... Russell said
most of the women on staff

203
00:10:37,434 --> 00:10:39,811
work in the dining hall.
You ever see them anyplace else?

204
00:10:39,936 --> 00:10:43,690
No... just a bunch of fat guys with pleated
shorts as far as the eye can see.

205
00:10:43,815 --> 00:10:46,902
That's a vivid image.
Okay, well, good luck with the FBI.

206
00:10:47,027 --> 00:10:51,323
You know, they had me dead to rights
and I beat 'em. I think you can, too.

207
00:10:51,448 --> 00:10:53,325
- What charge?
- Murder.

208
00:10:54,409 --> 00:10:56,912
- How'd you do that?
- Got to know the right people.

209
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:05,545
Our mom and dad were killed
in a car accident when we were little.

210
00:11:07,005 --> 00:11:11,509
The two of us spent years
in and out of foster homes.

211
00:11:11,635 --> 00:11:15,889
And one night, Madison...
It was, it was "Margaret" then...

212
00:11:17,349 --> 00:11:21,353
She ran away and I didn't hear
from her for months.

213
00:11:23,146 --> 00:11:25,774
Then, on my birthday,
she sent me $50.

214
00:11:27,567 --> 00:11:30,153
The next year she was paying for me
to go to boarding school.

215
00:11:33,448 --> 00:11:37,619
When I graduated, I asked her
if I could stay with her for a little while.

216
00:11:39,287 --> 00:11:42,207
- Where did the money come from?
- She worked.

217
00:11:43,333 --> 00:11:47,420
As a temp, at first and then
she started her own company.

218
00:11:48,338 --> 00:11:51,174
It helps executives
with their speaking skills.

219
00:11:51,508 --> 00:11:53,301
She e-mailed me this morning.

220
00:11:58,223 --> 00:12:01,768
"Dee, gentle reminder...
Fish must eat, or fish will die."

221
00:12:01,893 --> 00:12:03,645
- "Love you."
- She's in a cabin.

222
00:12:05,397 --> 00:12:08,191
Could someone have snuck her into
the Foragers' camp, a boyfriend, maybe?

223
00:12:09,484 --> 00:12:12,404
Madison didn't have any boyfriends.
She was gay.

224
00:12:13,154 --> 00:12:16,449
- Any girlfriends?
- Not serious ones.

225
00:12:16,574 --> 00:12:19,661
She worked too hard.
Their business wasn't doing well.

226
00:12:19,786 --> 00:12:22,956
Do you have any idea why she might
have been at the Foragers' club?

227
00:12:23,456 --> 00:12:25,959
No. She never mentioned it.

228
00:12:28,628 --> 00:12:31,589
We can talk to the waitstaff
after they're done with lunch service.

229
00:12:31,715 --> 00:12:33,425
According to the maître d',
it should be an hour and a half.

230
00:12:33,550 --> 00:12:36,261
Not to worry.
Excuse me? Miss?

231
00:12:36,386 --> 00:12:40,015
Could you pour my friend
some coffee, please? Thank you.

232
00:12:42,183 --> 00:12:44,936
- Tell Pike I say hi.
- How do you know it's Pike?

233
00:12:45,812 --> 00:12:48,148
"Jane says hi."

234
00:12:49,024 --> 00:12:51,401
- Yeah, he seems like a good guy.
- He is.

235
00:12:52,485 --> 00:12:54,571
Good, 'cause you deserve to be
with a good man.

236
00:12:55,822 --> 00:12:56,906
Thanks.

237
00:12:57,407 --> 00:13:01,119
Excuse me, ma'am.
I'd like to get some cream.

238
00:13:05,290 --> 00:13:06,541
Thank you.

239
00:13:08,168 --> 00:13:13,006
Excuse me. This... This fork is dirty.
There. You see that?

240
00:13:15,842 --> 00:13:18,511
- How about this one?
- Oh, yeah, that's clean. Thank you.

241
00:13:18,636 --> 00:13:20,180
Nice and clean. Excellent.

242
00:13:21,598 --> 00:13:24,726
- Okay. What is going on?
- The video Madison Pryce made

243
00:13:24,851 --> 00:13:26,936
showed that she stayed in a cabin
the night before she died.

244
00:13:27,062 --> 00:13:29,814
She was gay, so it was unlikely
a man's cabin...

245
00:13:29,939 --> 00:13:31,649
More likely a friend or a lover.

246
00:13:31,775 --> 00:13:33,151
She washed her hair

247
00:13:33,276 --> 00:13:37,822
so she probably borrowed the shampoo
from the other woman.

248
00:13:38,406 --> 00:13:41,326
None of these women here
used that shampoo.

249
00:13:41,451 --> 00:13:45,080
But by process of elimination, it leaves...
Excuse me, miss.

250
00:13:46,039 --> 00:13:47,749
Yes, ma'am, you.
Come here, please.

251
00:13:50,502 --> 00:13:54,756
Ginger citrus. Very nice.
Very nice.

252
00:13:54,881 --> 00:13:57,258
There we are.
You were with Madison Pryce last night.

253
00:13:57,717 --> 00:14:00,470
- Excuse me?
- Do you know Madison Pryce, ma'am?

254
00:14:03,014 --> 00:14:04,224
Oh, my God.

255
00:14:05,767 --> 00:14:08,061
It's her, isn't it?
She's the murdered woman.

256
00:14:09,479 --> 00:14:11,022
Oh, my God.

257
00:14:12,982 --> 00:14:15,443
And when was the last time
that you saw Madison?

258
00:14:17,654 --> 00:14:18,822
This morning.

259
00:14:20,615 --> 00:14:22,742
A little after 5:00...
that's when she left my cabin.

260
00:14:24,577 --> 00:14:26,037
We weren't a couple.

261
00:14:27,414 --> 00:14:29,332
I mean, we hooked up
when she came to the camp

262
00:14:29,457 --> 00:14:32,252
but it wasn't serious.
We just... Liked each other.

263
00:14:32,961 --> 00:14:36,339
- How did you meet?
- Last year...

264
00:14:36,673 --> 00:14:39,467
A late-night beer party for staff.

265
00:14:40,593 --> 00:14:43,430
She just roamed in.
I had no idea where she'd come from.

266
00:14:44,222 --> 00:14:46,224
- What was she doing there?
- Work.

267
00:14:47,892 --> 00:14:49,227
It was a gig for her.

268
00:14:49,894 --> 00:14:51,521
What kind of gig?

269
00:14:53,022 --> 00:14:56,151
- She was an escort.
- A... prostitute?

270
00:14:56,693 --> 00:14:58,194
She preferred "escort."

271
00:14:59,696 --> 00:15:03,116
She kept it quiet
so her sister wouldn't find out.

272
00:15:03,908 --> 00:15:06,870
The public-speaking business
was a cover story.

273
00:15:07,162 --> 00:15:10,165
- She was gay.
- It helped.

274
00:15:11,249 --> 00:15:13,001
She didn't take men
too seriously.

275
00:15:13,835 --> 00:15:18,423
- So, she was at the club to meet a client?
- Yeah, at the Fishing Camp.

276
00:15:19,007 --> 00:15:20,258
Fishing Camp?

277
00:15:21,259 --> 00:15:22,510
Okay.

278
00:15:24,679 --> 00:15:25,763
Thank you.

279
00:15:27,974 --> 00:15:29,267
That was Fischer.

280
00:15:29,767 --> 00:15:33,563
According to Pam Madison Pryce
worked as a prostitute

281
00:15:33,688 --> 00:15:38,610
at a brothel they maintain on the grounds
called the Fishing Camp.

282
00:15:43,521 --> 00:15:45,273
So you run a brothel at the Foragers.

283
00:15:45,398 --> 00:15:48,943
The club provides a service...
at not one I avail myself of.

284
00:15:49,068 --> 00:15:53,114
Sure. And it's called...
Fishing Camp.

285
00:15:53,239 --> 00:15:56,617
In my father's and grandfather's time
it actually was, used for fishing.

286
00:15:56,742 --> 00:15:58,911
- But not anymore.
- We had a problem.

287
00:16:00,204 --> 00:16:03,666
In years past people would go to a house
about 20 miles away from the camp

288
00:16:03,791 --> 00:16:06,127
when they... wanted
that kind of thing.

289
00:16:07,044 --> 00:16:10,298
But there was drunkenness,
accidents, arrests.

290
00:16:10,840 --> 00:16:13,426
Not a healthy situation
in the age of the camera phone.

291
00:16:14,302 --> 00:16:15,970
So Kilgallen...

292
00:16:17,346 --> 00:16:20,933
came up with the idea of moving
the enterprise onto our grounds

293
00:16:21,058 --> 00:16:23,478
as a way of avoiding the difficulties.
And it worked.

294
00:16:23,769 --> 00:16:26,522
- We've had no problems since then.
- Except a murder.

295
00:16:27,106 --> 00:16:28,399
Yes. Obviously.

296
00:16:28,524 --> 00:16:32,236
- So, it was Kilgallen's operation?
- Yeah. Completely.

297
00:16:32,361 --> 00:16:37,074
He brings the women up on the weekends
usually one or two at a time.

298
00:16:37,200 --> 00:16:39,076
Got them on some sort of rotation.

299
00:16:39,202 --> 00:16:42,538
The Foragers', by the way,
derives no income from this.

300
00:16:43,164 --> 00:16:44,707
I was very firm about that.

301
00:16:44,832 --> 00:16:47,710
- Did you know Madison Pryce?
- No.

302
00:16:47,835 --> 00:16:50,671
According to Pam Parsons
Madison visited the camp several times.

303
00:16:51,547 --> 00:16:53,591
- You never saw her?
- Well, that wasn't your question.

304
00:16:53,716 --> 00:16:57,637
You asked me if I knew her, which I did not
by any reasonable definition of the term.

305
00:17:00,097 --> 00:17:05,478
Upon reconsideration, I think it's possible
that I did see her once or twice.

306
00:17:05,603 --> 00:17:06,938
We never spoke.

307
00:17:07,063 --> 00:17:09,524
Now, you told Jane
you didn't recognize her picture.

308
00:17:10,775 --> 00:17:13,236
Not many people can get an evasion
like that past Jane.

309
00:17:14,195 --> 00:17:16,489
- Your point being...
- You must be a very good liar.

310
00:17:17,031 --> 00:17:18,950
Where were you from 6:00 to 8:00
this morning?

311
00:17:20,159 --> 00:17:21,619
I was asleep.

312
00:17:26,207 --> 00:17:27,333
Whoo!

313
00:17:27,792 --> 00:17:30,545
Hey! Look who I found.

314
00:17:30,795 --> 00:17:33,756
- What are you doing here?
- Well, you have poked the bear.

315
00:17:33,881 --> 00:17:35,174
After Russell was questioned

316
00:17:35,299 --> 00:17:37,426
he called a few high-powered people,
and they called me

317
00:17:37,552 --> 00:17:39,720
so I figured I'd come down
and check everything out.

318
00:17:40,638 --> 00:17:43,391
- Where's Kilgallen?
- I don't know. He's not in his office.

319
00:17:43,516 --> 00:17:46,686
His laptop isn't there, either.
Camp security is looking for him.

320
00:17:46,811 --> 00:17:49,772
He's on the run.
Not the type to pack a bag.

321
00:17:49,897 --> 00:17:52,024
All right. Well, I'll have Fischer
start a search.

322
00:17:52,358 --> 00:17:55,570
- So, this is the Fishing Camp, huh?
- Madison used cabin 9.

323
00:18:02,577 --> 00:18:06,956
Pam Parsons says that Madison left her
cabin at 5:00 this morning to come here.

324
00:18:07,123 --> 00:18:08,666
That was the last time
anybody saw her.

325
00:18:09,166 --> 00:18:11,961
- I don't think she died here.
- What makes you say that?

326
00:18:12,503 --> 00:18:13,963
Well, there's nothing out of place.

327
00:18:14,088 --> 00:18:16,424
There's no scratches on the floor
from furniture sliding.

328
00:18:16,549 --> 00:18:18,050
A fight would have left marks.

329
00:18:18,175 --> 00:18:20,886
Well, I would have called it just a hunch,
but that works, too.

330
00:18:21,012 --> 00:18:23,222
Maybe it started in here
and then went outside.

331
00:18:23,347 --> 00:18:26,058
Either way, the killer had to have used
something to move the body.

332
00:18:26,183 --> 00:18:28,060
There's no way they carried it
to where it was found.

333
00:18:28,185 --> 00:18:29,687
There are a lot of golf carts here.

334
00:18:30,229 --> 00:18:32,440
Get forensics up here.
Have them test the golf carts.

335
00:18:32,565 --> 00:18:35,985
Tell them to bring the m-vac system.
That'll dig DNA fragments out of anything.

336
00:18:36,110 --> 00:18:39,363
And if Madison's DNA is on that golf cart
then the killer's might be, too.

337
00:18:40,072 --> 00:18:41,449
What is that?

338
00:18:44,577 --> 00:18:45,620
Wait a minute.

339
00:18:50,082 --> 00:18:51,459
It's a golf-ball marker.

340
00:18:51,751 --> 00:18:53,961
You use it to, uh,
place your ball on the Green.

341
00:18:54,086 --> 00:18:56,422
whoever visited this room
must've been a serious linksman.

342
00:18:56,881 --> 00:18:59,300
But a place like this, ah,
that must be a lot of people.

343
00:18:59,425 --> 00:19:00,968
I think we can narrow it down.

344
00:19:08,768 --> 00:19:11,270
Grant Kimura.
Dennis Abbott, FBI.

345
00:19:11,687 --> 00:19:14,607
I'm a fan. I saw you play
in Augusta last summer.

346
00:19:14,732 --> 00:19:16,400
That putt that you made
on the seventh...

347
00:19:16,525 --> 00:19:18,778
- Oh, that was amazing.
- Amazing.

348
00:19:18,903 --> 00:19:21,614
Patrick Jane.
I don't know anything about golf.

349
00:19:21,739 --> 00:19:24,408
Okay. Cool.
What's this about?

350
00:19:24,950 --> 00:19:28,162
All security told me was some guys
from the FBI wanted to talk.

351
00:19:28,287 --> 00:19:29,830
I got a massage in 15.

352
00:19:29,955 --> 00:19:31,957
Well, we found something
that belongs to you.

353
00:19:33,167 --> 00:19:35,461
- Where'd you get that?
- Over at the Fishing Camp.

354
00:19:35,586 --> 00:19:38,089
You know it, don't you?
Sure you do.

355
00:19:38,214 --> 00:19:40,424
No, man. I don't know
what you're talking about.

356
00:19:40,549 --> 00:19:43,386
- Plays golf better than he lies, huh?
- Who are you, again?

357
00:19:43,511 --> 00:19:47,348
We found it in a cabin that is used
by a woman named Madison Pryce.

358
00:19:47,973 --> 00:19:50,476
It must have just fallen out of your pocket
while you were there.

359
00:19:50,893 --> 00:19:53,187
There are a million of those.
Could belong to anyone.

360
00:19:53,312 --> 00:19:55,731
Yeah, well, the Fishing Camp's
for a certain kind of man...

361
00:19:55,856 --> 00:20:01,070
Pampered, hedonistic, self-centered.
Certainly screams out "professional athlete."

362
00:20:01,195 --> 00:20:03,698
Now, we're talking to a lot of people
about the Fishing Camp.

363
00:20:04,156 --> 00:20:06,826
If you deny being there
and we find out that you lied

364
00:20:06,951 --> 00:20:08,285
that's obstruction of justice.

365
00:20:08,786 --> 00:20:10,287
It's worth thinking about.

366
00:20:15,710 --> 00:20:17,086
I'm married, okay?

367
00:20:17,628 --> 00:20:22,299
Two little boys. And my endorsements
they require a certain... Public image.

368
00:20:22,550 --> 00:20:25,052
Oh, we get it. We get it.
You're in a tough place.

369
00:20:25,720 --> 00:20:27,722
Grant, what's going on?

370
00:20:27,847 --> 00:20:30,683
- Are these people hassling you?
- Man, Greg, thanks for coming.

371
00:20:30,808 --> 00:20:33,102
I heard from a security guy the FBI
wanted to talk to you.

372
00:20:33,227 --> 00:20:34,729
You guys are FBI, right?

373
00:20:35,146 --> 00:20:37,398
Well, I'm Gregory Dyer.
I'm Mr. Kimura's Attorney.

374
00:20:37,523 --> 00:20:40,985
So whatever conversation or interrogation
you have going on here is over

375
00:20:41,110 --> 00:20:42,695
because he's not
gonna say anything.

376
00:20:42,820 --> 00:20:44,321
And the idea
that you would question him

377
00:20:44,447 --> 00:20:47,158
without an attorney present
is a shocking professional lapse.

378
00:20:47,283 --> 00:20:49,660
- I'll be complaining to your superiors.
- So frisky.

379
00:20:49,785 --> 00:20:54,039
Oh, we were just talking to Mr. Kimura
about a woman named Madison Pryce

380
00:20:54,165 --> 00:20:56,041
who was killed earlier this morning.

381
00:20:56,167 --> 00:21:00,504
Now, we believe Mr. Kimura saw Ms. Pryce
within the last 24 hours.

382
00:21:00,629 --> 00:21:03,048
So anything that he saw or heard

383
00:21:03,174 --> 00:21:05,551
that would be a great help
to finding her killer

384
00:21:05,843 --> 00:21:08,637
which we assume
he wants us to do.

385
00:21:08,763 --> 00:21:11,515
- Nice try.
- Okay, Abbott, let's go.

386
00:21:11,640 --> 00:21:15,394
It's obvious we're no match
for the keen legal mind of Mr. Dyer here.

387
00:21:15,519 --> 00:21:17,813
You're not actually a member
of the Foragers', are you?

388
00:21:17,938 --> 00:21:19,607
No, I'm here as grant's guest.

389
00:21:20,941 --> 00:21:23,360
Yeah, I didn't think so.
You're too, sweaty.

390
00:21:23,486 --> 00:21:25,654
Why don't you have one
of those guest-badge thingies?

391
00:21:26,405 --> 00:21:28,866
I'm what's called a residential guest.
We don't do that.

392
00:21:28,991 --> 00:21:31,035
So concerned about status here,
aren't they?

393
00:21:31,160 --> 00:21:32,953
By the way, your collar is frayed.

394
00:21:33,078 --> 00:21:34,914
You might want to bill him
for a new shirt.

395
00:21:40,419 --> 00:21:43,255
Still no sign of Kilgallen.
Fischer's put out a bolo.

396
00:21:43,798 --> 00:21:47,718
Call the office. Get as many agents up
here as we can find to sweep the woods.

397
00:21:48,177 --> 00:21:50,346
We got to find this guy.
How's it going with the golf cart?

398
00:21:50,471 --> 00:21:52,014
The techs are working on it now.

399
00:21:52,139 --> 00:21:54,266
One of the carts
was methodically cleaned...

400
00:21:54,391 --> 00:21:57,144
No fingerprints, no dust. They don't
think they'll find any DNA, either.

401
00:21:57,269 --> 00:21:58,395
Well, that's the death cart.

402
00:21:58,521 --> 00:22:01,732
The weird thing is, the techs say that
the cart was washed within the last hour.

403
00:22:02,024 --> 00:22:03,108
It's still wet.

404
00:22:03,234 --> 00:22:06,612
- Why would the killer wait until now?
- That does seem odd.

405
00:22:07,154 --> 00:22:10,074
All right. Well, ask around. See if anyone
saw who cleaned the golf cart.

406
00:22:10,199 --> 00:22:11,242
Sure.

407
00:22:12,326 --> 00:22:15,913
- Excuse me. Are you Abbott?
- Marvin. Marvin Gryska.

408
00:22:16,038 --> 00:22:18,749
This is a... this is
a very energetic Forager.

409
00:22:18,874 --> 00:22:20,042
You are Abbott with the FBI, right?

410
00:22:20,167 --> 00:22:23,879
- Yes, I am supervisory agent Dennis Abbott.
- Okay. I need to talk to you.

411
00:22:24,129 --> 00:22:25,381
- I'm busy.
- Have fun.

412
00:22:25,506 --> 00:22:27,800
Just a minute of your time. Please.

413
00:22:35,391 --> 00:22:36,809
Okay, what can I do for you?

414
00:22:37,476 --> 00:22:40,145
I made a couple of calls.
That guy Jane who was just here...

415
00:22:40,271 --> 00:22:42,606
He told me he beat a murder charge
the FBI had on him.

416
00:22:42,940 --> 00:22:44,984
You're the agent that made
that deal for him, aren't you?

417
00:22:45,693 --> 00:22:48,070
I don't know where you get your
information from, Mr. Gryska

418
00:22:48,195 --> 00:22:50,698
- but I can't discuss that with you.
- It's okay. My information's good.

419
00:22:50,823 --> 00:22:51,949
You're in Austin.

420
00:22:52,074 --> 00:22:53,868
You have an agent there
named Matthews.

421
00:22:53,993 --> 00:22:55,953
Jim Matthews
is in a different division.

422
00:22:56,996 --> 00:22:58,747
Mr. Gryska,
I can't help you.

423
00:22:58,873 --> 00:23:00,416
You just asked
what could you do for me.

424
00:23:00,541 --> 00:23:01,584
That was a figure of speech.

425
00:23:01,709 --> 00:23:04,587
I just heard your agent Matthews
has frozen my assets.

426
00:23:04,712 --> 00:23:06,088
He's locked my money away.

427
00:23:06,213 --> 00:23:08,799
And why?
Apparently, he thinks I'm a flight risk.

428
00:23:09,425 --> 00:23:11,010
- Are you?
- No!

429
00:23:11,302 --> 00:23:13,429
Because the case against me
is gonna collapse.

430
00:23:13,554 --> 00:23:15,514
What was my crime?
I made money. I got rich.

431
00:23:15,639 --> 00:23:16,807
And I made other people rich...

432
00:23:16,932 --> 00:23:18,893
And not blue-chip types,
like these guys.

433
00:23:19,393 --> 00:23:21,645
Teachers, firemen, cops...
people just like you.

434
00:23:24,398 --> 00:23:25,649
What's your point?

435
00:23:27,610 --> 00:23:29,653
Matthews is being unreasonable.

436
00:23:30,404 --> 00:23:35,326
I'm looking for someone reasonable
from the FBI to mediate my situation

437
00:23:35,451 --> 00:23:38,037
and I'd be very grateful
if I could find that person.

438
00:23:38,162 --> 00:23:42,291
- So you're offering me a bribe?
- Well, no, no. No. That would be illegal.

439
00:23:43,167 --> 00:23:45,252
I'm just looking to put
this mess behind me

440
00:23:45,377 --> 00:23:48,130
so I can get back to the business
of making people rich.

441
00:23:48,464 --> 00:23:53,802
- Can we discuss that, agent Abbott?
- I'll get back to you.

442
00:23:54,303 --> 00:23:55,971
Okay. Great.
Soon.

443
00:23:57,222 --> 00:23:58,349
But great.

444
00:24:00,059 --> 00:24:03,687
I came looking for you but they said
you're still in the woods. I'm disappointed.

445
00:24:03,812 --> 00:24:06,690
I'm sorry. I don't know
when I'm gonna be back.

446
00:24:06,815 --> 00:24:08,776
Could we do dinner
tomorrow night, maybe?

447
00:24:09,026 --> 00:24:10,903
No problem. What's holding you up?

448
00:24:11,028 --> 00:24:15,157
This guy named Kilgallen took off.
He's nowhere to be found at the camp.

449
00:24:15,282 --> 00:24:17,868
We put a bolo out on him,
but that didn't turn anything up.

450
00:24:18,327 --> 00:24:21,497
We figure he rabbited into the woods.
It's gonna be dark in an hour

451
00:24:21,622 --> 00:24:24,083
and there is no way
we're gonna find him at night.

452
00:24:24,333 --> 00:24:26,877
Have your people tried accessing
a forestry satellite?

453
00:24:27,002 --> 00:24:29,672
They're imaging that area all the time
looking for fire outbreaks.

454
00:24:29,797 --> 00:24:31,757
That is an excellent idea.

455
00:24:31,882 --> 00:24:35,469
I used one once to find a guy who was running
with some stolen Frederic Remingtons.

456
00:24:35,594 --> 00:24:38,389
Check you out...
good-looking and helpful.

457
00:24:40,224 --> 00:24:41,558
- Bye.
- "Bye."

458
00:24:46,438 --> 00:24:48,190
We're at the map coordinates
you gave us.

459
00:24:48,732 --> 00:24:52,111
Okay, according to the satellite image
there should be a clearing ahead.

460
00:24:52,403 --> 00:24:56,240
Yeah, looks like there's something there.
Is it maybe a... a service vehicle?

461
00:24:56,490 --> 00:24:57,866
All right. Got it.

462
00:25:23,350 --> 00:25:26,353
- That our guy?
- Yeah. That's Kilgallen.

463
00:25:35,886 --> 00:25:38,639
The techs and M.E. are
finishing up at the crime scene.

464
00:25:38,764 --> 00:25:40,265
The gun that Kilgallen was shot with

465
00:25:40,390 --> 00:25:42,768
appears to have been taken
from the club's trapshooting range

466
00:25:42,893 --> 00:25:44,519
but there's no prints on it.

467
00:25:44,770 --> 00:25:48,106
- Any sign of Kilgallen's laptop?
- None. The killer must have taken it.

468
00:25:48,440 --> 00:25:51,109
Well, it seems pretty clear
that Kilgallen was murdered

469
00:25:51,234 --> 00:25:53,403
because he knew
who Madison Pryce's killer is.

470
00:25:53,528 --> 00:25:56,406
So, tomorrow, we go at the club members
about Kilgallen, right?

471
00:25:56,531 --> 00:25:58,450
Does anyone at the camp
know he's dead?

472
00:25:58,575 --> 00:25:59,785
No, not yet.

473
00:26:01,036 --> 00:26:03,664
- I wouldn't be too quick to mention it.
- Why not?

474
00:26:03,789 --> 00:26:05,540
One dead body
makes people antsy.

475
00:26:05,666 --> 00:26:08,251
They hear about Kilgallen,
the place will be empty by dinner.

476
00:26:08,377 --> 00:26:10,420
- So what do we do?
- Good question.

477
00:26:10,545 --> 00:26:13,548
Well, I'll let you people think about that.
I got to draft a memo to I.A.

478
00:26:13,674 --> 00:26:15,425
Internal affairs?
What's going on with them?

479
00:26:16,093 --> 00:26:19,846
Marvin Gryska all but offered me a bribe
to release his frozen assets.

480
00:26:20,681 --> 00:26:22,849
- Oh, yeah?
- Well, he's desperate.

481
00:26:23,141 --> 00:26:24,476
He's looking at 20 years.

482
00:26:24,601 --> 00:26:26,812
Probably why he wants me
to release his assets...

483
00:26:26,937 --> 00:26:28,230
So he can
run across the border.

484
00:26:29,106 --> 00:26:32,109
- What'd you tell him?
- What I was supposed to tell him...

485
00:26:32,234 --> 00:26:33,360
That I'll think about it.

486
00:26:33,652 --> 00:26:35,612
And then I'll get him
to make that offer on tape

487
00:26:35,737 --> 00:26:39,533
and we'll nail that fool's ass
for attempting to bribe a federal official.

488
00:26:39,783 --> 00:26:41,743
Or you could not do any of that.

489
00:26:43,036 --> 00:26:44,621
Why? Because I have a better plan.

490
00:26:44,746 --> 00:26:46,748
Were we able to get anything
out of Madison Pryce's phone?

491
00:26:46,873 --> 00:26:47,958
Not yet. They're still working on it.

492
00:26:48,083 --> 00:26:50,043
We're gonna need that phone
and a little help from Wylie.

493
00:26:50,168 --> 00:26:53,880
Is it possible to get the camp on lockdown...
No one in or out?

494
00:26:55,132 --> 00:26:58,468
I might be able to claim the investigation
requires it for a couple of hours

495
00:26:58,593 --> 00:27:00,429
- but no more than that.
- Plenty long enough.

496
00:27:11,732 --> 00:27:12,774
Hey.

497
00:27:13,233 --> 00:27:15,444
Morning. What's up?

498
00:27:16,319 --> 00:27:19,156
- I need you to do something for me.
- Something shady for Jane?

499
00:27:19,531 --> 00:27:21,700
- Why do you ask?
- Because people only talk to me like this

500
00:27:21,825 --> 00:27:23,577
When we're doing
something shady for Jane.

501
00:27:23,744 --> 00:27:25,037
I need you to create an electronic trail

502
00:27:25,162 --> 00:27:28,331
that makes it look like the FBI just released
millions of dollars in frozen assets.

503
00:27:28,832 --> 00:27:30,333
You mean a DNS hijacking?

504
00:27:32,127 --> 00:27:33,545
When... when you go
to a bank's website

505
00:27:33,670 --> 00:27:35,797
it redirects you to a phony version
of the same site.

506
00:27:35,922 --> 00:27:37,174
You can make it look
like anything you want.

507
00:27:37,299 --> 00:27:39,217
Yes... that. And yes, it's for Jane.

508
00:27:40,677 --> 00:27:42,137
- Okay. Sure.
- You can do it?

509
00:27:42,596 --> 00:27:45,140
- With my eyes closed.
- Good man.

510
00:28:00,572 --> 00:28:04,951
- Got your message. What's up?
- I thought about your offer.

511
00:28:06,244 --> 00:28:08,538
You want your assets
made available to you, right?

512
00:28:08,955 --> 00:28:11,416
So you can take a little trip
before the trial starts?

513
00:28:13,210 --> 00:28:16,463
The cost is 30%
of whatever funds I free up.

514
00:28:19,299 --> 00:28:21,885
At this moment, I'm gonna ask...
are you wearing a wire?

515
00:28:22,010 --> 00:28:23,095
No.

516
00:28:23,595 --> 00:28:26,264
'Cause if you are and you lied just now,
this is entrapment.

517
00:28:26,973 --> 00:28:29,142
30... that's the price.

518
00:28:34,856 --> 00:28:35,982
20.

519
00:28:40,153 --> 00:28:42,197
I am the only one
that can help you right now.

520
00:28:43,323 --> 00:28:45,492
If you don't believe me,
ask Patrick Jane.

521
00:28:46,201 --> 00:28:47,244
30.

522
00:28:50,956 --> 00:28:52,040
30.

523
00:28:52,958 --> 00:28:54,167
All right.

524
00:29:10,684 --> 00:29:13,478
Your funds are now set free,
minus my percentage.

525
00:29:14,146 --> 00:29:16,565
Jim Matthews won't know
for at least three days.

526
00:29:20,569 --> 00:29:23,071
- That's it?
- Check your account.

527
00:29:23,405 --> 00:29:24,906
Nice doing business with you.

528
00:29:31,872 --> 00:29:33,331
He just checked the website.

529
00:29:33,915 --> 00:29:35,250
Everything looks good.

530
00:29:36,376 --> 00:29:38,336
- Gryska bought it.
- Good.

531
00:29:39,337 --> 00:29:41,339
- Okay.
- Mr. Jane.

532
00:29:42,924 --> 00:29:45,385
- Any sign of Kilgallen?
- Afraid not.

533
00:29:45,594 --> 00:29:48,013
Well, find him soon, please.
I'd very much like to fire him.

534
00:29:48,138 --> 00:29:51,516
Sure. You, uh, look a little put off.
What seems to be the trouble?

535
00:29:51,641 --> 00:29:55,187
Well, thanks to your FBI blockade
our speaker can't get into the camp.

536
00:29:55,729 --> 00:29:57,189
We're gonna have to
postpone the talk.

537
00:29:57,314 --> 00:30:01,610
Oh. I a... I am sorry. That is...
that is... that's very disappointing.

538
00:30:01,735 --> 00:30:03,695
Yeah. People are
gonna be disappointed.

539
00:30:05,238 --> 00:30:07,574
Well, you know,
I used to be a magician.

540
00:30:08,450 --> 00:30:10,035
- Really?
- Yeah.

541
00:30:12,287 --> 00:30:13,496
I was pretty good at it, actually.

542
00:30:13,622 --> 00:30:16,374
I-I could try and entertain the troops
for you briefly

543
00:30:16,499 --> 00:30:18,168
if you'd like, if you twisted my arm.

544
00:30:18,293 --> 00:30:20,712
I mean, it's the least I could do
after all the trouble I've caused.

545
00:30:20,837 --> 00:30:25,133
- Yeah, I'm not so sure about that.
- Just, this... is this yours?

546
00:30:25,258 --> 00:30:27,385
I mean, I'd only charge you
like a dollar.

547
00:30:34,017 --> 00:30:35,602
My God, that was incredible.

548
00:30:36,478 --> 00:30:40,273
I guess the members might be interested
in... seeing something, yeah.

549
00:30:40,398 --> 00:30:43,944
Oh, sure they would. I've got some props,
but I can take care of that.

550
00:30:44,069 --> 00:30:45,862
We'll have some fun. Okay?

551
00:30:46,738 --> 00:30:49,241
Put a smile on that face, huh?
See you later.

552
00:30:53,870 --> 00:30:56,248
Okay, people.
Let's get together, shall we?

553
00:31:00,043 --> 00:31:01,253
Thanks for coming.

554
00:31:01,378 --> 00:31:04,506
- It's important nobody overhear us.
- Where were you?

555
00:31:04,756 --> 00:31:07,300
- I was arranging to perform this afternoon.
- Why?

556
00:31:07,801 --> 00:31:09,344
Just a minute.
Do you have Madison's phone?

557
00:31:09,469 --> 00:31:10,595
Here.

558
00:31:10,720 --> 00:31:12,639
The techs say that there's an audio file

559
00:31:12,764 --> 00:31:15,684
recorded at 5:52 the morning
of Madison's murder.

560
00:31:15,809 --> 00:31:18,270
Apparently, she recorded
a message for herself.

561
00:31:18,603 --> 00:31:20,647
That was around
the same time she was killed.

562
00:31:21,147 --> 00:31:23,149
What if she got audio
of the... of the killer?

563
00:31:23,441 --> 00:31:25,944
- What's on it?
- They don't know. It's too damaged.

564
00:31:26,069 --> 00:31:28,613
They can repair it,
but it'll take weeks.

565
00:31:28,738 --> 00:31:31,950
- No need. We can identify the killer today.
- How?

566
00:31:32,200 --> 00:31:34,286
I'm gonna do a routine onstage
with four objects

567
00:31:34,411 --> 00:31:36,997
that I will say I got from the club's
lost and found.

568
00:31:37,122 --> 00:31:39,666
One of those will be this phone.

569
00:31:40,458 --> 00:31:44,462
When the killer sees it
he will identify it as Madison's.

570
00:31:44,587 --> 00:31:46,381
And think that there's evidence on it.

571
00:31:46,506 --> 00:31:47,966
Well, he doesn't know
that the FBI has it.

572
00:31:48,091 --> 00:31:49,384
He's gonna think
it's in the lost and found.

573
00:31:49,509 --> 00:31:53,346
And after the show, he will go to the
lost and found and try to take it back.

574
00:31:53,471 --> 00:31:55,307
- That's when we get him.
- Nice.

575
00:31:55,432 --> 00:31:57,767
- Unless he doesn't try to take it.
- Oh, he will.

576
00:31:58,268 --> 00:32:02,355
It's like offering honey to a hungry bear.
He won't be able to resist.

577
00:32:09,502 --> 00:32:14,090
Now I'm gonna place this card
back into the deck. Do a little dance.

578
00:32:14,715 --> 00:32:17,760
And if you look in your right pant pocket
I think you might find...

579
00:32:18,678 --> 00:32:19,720
What do you got in there?

580
00:32:20,638 --> 00:32:22,223
How did you do that, Jim?

581
00:32:24,058 --> 00:32:26,519
Thank you. Thank you.
Now please take a seat.

582
00:32:27,437 --> 00:32:28,771
I'm gonna do one more thing for you.

583
00:32:28,896 --> 00:32:33,818
I'm gonna need the assistance of the
irreplaceable special agent Teresa Lisbon.

584
00:32:38,948 --> 00:32:42,410
Earlier today, I approached
four random members of the club.

585
00:32:42,660 --> 00:32:44,787
Are they here?
Could you raise your hands?

586
00:32:44,912 --> 00:32:48,791
Yes. I asked each of these men
to go to the club lost and found

587
00:32:48,916 --> 00:32:51,210
and to choose one item
and place it in a cardboard box

588
00:32:51,335 --> 00:32:52,837
and then seal that box.

589
00:32:52,962 --> 00:32:54,505
- Do we have the box here?
- Yeah.

590
00:32:54,797 --> 00:32:55,923
Thank you.

591
00:32:57,425 --> 00:33:01,179
Now, just to be clear,
neither I nor agent Lisbon

592
00:33:01,471 --> 00:33:04,265
saw what you picked
or know the contents of this box.

593
00:33:04,390 --> 00:33:05,683
- Is that correct?
- That's correct.

594
00:33:05,808 --> 00:33:06,851
Excellent.

595
00:33:06,976 --> 00:33:12,023
Now, without the benefit of sight
I am going to attempt to identify each object

596
00:33:12,148 --> 00:33:14,484
as agent Lisbon holds it up.

597
00:33:19,030 --> 00:33:20,448
- Are we ready?
- Yep.

598
00:33:21,407 --> 00:33:22,992
- Here's the box.
- Okay, no, not here.

599
00:33:23,117 --> 00:33:25,661
- I have a chair back here.
- Okay, just over... Yeah, it's right here.

600
00:33:25,786 --> 00:33:26,829
- You're doing good, Lisbon.
- Okay.

601
00:33:26,954 --> 00:33:28,664
You're doing good.
Just stay calm. Relax.

602
00:33:28,789 --> 00:33:30,708
Just let your beauty show.

603
00:33:31,959 --> 00:33:35,171
- Okay, are we ready, agent Lisbon?
- Yes, we are.

604
00:33:35,296 --> 00:33:36,714
Then let's begin!

605
00:33:38,466 --> 00:33:39,675
First item.

606
00:33:41,636 --> 00:33:43,846
What am I holding
in my hand?

607
00:33:46,140 --> 00:33:48,392
It takes a licking
but keeps on ticking.

608
00:33:49,435 --> 00:33:51,562
- A watch?
- Correct.

609
00:33:55,816 --> 00:33:56,901
Next!

610
00:33:57,443 --> 00:33:59,946
What am I holding
in my hand now?

611
00:34:00,530 --> 00:34:04,534
They say it's mightier than the...

612
00:34:05,409 --> 00:34:08,287
- is it a pen?
- Yes, it is.

613
00:34:10,081 --> 00:34:11,457
Two for two.

614
00:34:12,208 --> 00:34:13,459
What is in my hand now?

615
00:34:14,460 --> 00:34:18,005
This one is difficult.
If I could only just...

616
00:34:18,130 --> 00:34:19,382
See.

617
00:34:20,299 --> 00:34:23,052
- Glasses?
- You are correct.

618
00:34:24,679 --> 00:34:25,972
Last one.

619
00:34:27,848 --> 00:34:31,686
- Can you figure this one out?
- It's a word.

620
00:34:32,436 --> 00:34:33,896
"Harm." No.

621
00:34:34,605 --> 00:34:37,358
"Hurt"? "Hurt"... no.
"Broken."

622
00:34:37,483 --> 00:34:38,943
Broken...
Broken... Cell.

623
00:34:39,068 --> 00:34:40,820
Broken...
is it a broken cellphone?

624
00:34:40,945 --> 00:34:42,363
Yes, it is.

625
00:34:44,740 --> 00:34:45,992
Thank you!

626
00:34:46,117 --> 00:34:49,453
Thank you. Really, thank you.
And... Thank you.

627
00:34:53,040 --> 00:34:56,919
A big Foragers' thank you to Mr. Jane
and special agent Lisbon.

628
00:34:57,044 --> 00:34:59,922
Terrific. Terrific.
Beautiful.

629
00:35:00,965 --> 00:35:02,049
Good to see you.

630
00:35:03,092 --> 00:35:04,260
How are you doing?

631
00:35:50,556 --> 00:35:53,517
I know you can hear me...
And see me...

632
00:35:55,394 --> 00:35:57,146
'cause you've got a camera in here.

633
00:35:58,814 --> 00:36:01,651
You did it so you can blackmail
one of Madison's clients.

634
00:36:03,235 --> 00:36:06,572
It's how you also knew
that you needed to clean that golf cart

635
00:36:06,697 --> 00:36:08,324
"before the tech got here."

636
00:36:08,449 --> 00:36:11,327
"We only talked about it in this room
so we know you heard."

637
00:36:12,536 --> 00:36:15,164
It's how you also knew
not to take the phone.

638
00:36:23,339 --> 00:36:24,799
"But I still got it."

639
00:36:25,341 --> 00:36:30,054
And it still has evidence
that will implicate you.

640
00:36:31,222 --> 00:36:34,892
And given the exchange
that I know you witnessed in here

641
00:36:36,519 --> 00:36:38,688
between me and Mr. Gryska

642
00:36:39,980 --> 00:36:44,026
I'm pretty sure that you know
that I am willing to make a deal.

643
00:36:45,277 --> 00:36:46,821
"I'll give you 10 minutes."

644
00:37:17,935 --> 00:37:19,437
What kind of deal
are we talking about?

645
00:37:22,064 --> 00:37:23,649
None at all.

646
00:37:31,615 --> 00:37:32,742
Go!

647
00:37:35,870 --> 00:37:38,706
What are you d...
I didn't do anything!

648
00:37:39,039 --> 00:37:41,417
Et voilà.
A honey bear.

649
00:37:44,879 --> 00:37:47,631
We found Kilgallen's laptop
in your room, Mr. Dyer.

650
00:37:49,800 --> 00:37:52,720
Your client Grant Kimura
had scheduled a session

651
00:37:52,845 --> 00:37:54,889
to see Madison Pryce at 10:00
on the day she died.

652
00:37:56,098 --> 00:37:57,725
You were gonna blackmail him,
weren't you?

653
00:37:59,143 --> 00:38:04,356
There are some... irregularities
in grant's accounts.

654
00:38:04,857 --> 00:38:06,192
I could explain everything.

655
00:38:08,152 --> 00:38:10,279
But I needed some leverage.

656
00:38:12,948 --> 00:38:14,700
When grant invited me...

657
00:38:16,494 --> 00:38:18,162
To the Foragers' again

658
00:38:18,287 --> 00:38:23,125
I thought about... The girl in cabin 9.
He'd been with her before.

659
00:38:24,043 --> 00:38:26,086
So I cut a deal with Kilgallen.

660
00:38:27,296 --> 00:38:30,424
- He let you put the camera in the cabin.
- For a fee.

661
00:38:30,549 --> 00:38:32,301
Kilgallen always had his hand out.

662
00:38:33,511 --> 00:38:36,972
But when I went to the cabin
to put the camera in that morning...

663
00:38:38,641 --> 00:38:40,142
the girl came back early.

664
00:38:45,981 --> 00:38:47,233
What are you doing?

665
00:38:51,195 --> 00:38:52,404
"I went after her."

666
00:38:54,114 --> 00:38:56,909
"I was just trying to...
Make her stop."

667
00:39:07,294 --> 00:39:10,422
"And she just...
Stopped moving."

668
00:39:12,758 --> 00:39:15,302
So, you left kimura's
golf-ball marker in there

669
00:39:15,427 --> 00:39:18,347
- to throw suspicion on him.
- Yes.

670
00:39:18,973 --> 00:39:22,726
I thought that if there was legal trouble
I could help him, he would be grateful

671
00:39:22,852 --> 00:39:24,395
and he would forget
the financial stuff.

672
00:39:24,520 --> 00:39:27,273
- Kilgallen figured out what had happened.
- Right away.

673
00:39:27,398 --> 00:39:30,609
And he put his hand out...
Said that he'd be quiet if I paid.

674
00:39:31,402 --> 00:39:33,195
And we met to settle the terms.

675
00:39:47,376 --> 00:39:49,461
That thing with the phone
was a trick, wasn't it?

676
00:39:49,712 --> 00:39:51,213
Oh, yes.

677
00:39:51,755 --> 00:39:53,340
There's nothing on that phone.

678
00:39:53,591 --> 00:39:55,217
And Gryska never got
any money, did he?

679
00:39:55,342 --> 00:39:57,761
No. Gryska's under arrest...
just like you.

680
00:40:04,768 --> 00:40:07,187
- I'm sorry we had to put this off.
- Oh, it's okay.

681
00:40:07,313 --> 00:40:09,398
I took another girl out to dinner
last night instead.

682
00:40:09,899 --> 00:40:11,066
- Really?
- Yeah.

683
00:40:11,191 --> 00:40:12,985
You trying to make me jealous?

684
00:40:13,110 --> 00:40:15,237
- What's her name?
- Gladys...

685
00:40:17,156 --> 00:40:22,703
- E-eg-egbert.
- Gladys... Egbert? Hmm? Yeah?

686
00:40:22,828 --> 00:40:24,663
That's the best I could do
on the spur of the moment.

687
00:40:24,788 --> 00:40:26,874
You are a terrible liar.

688
00:40:26,999 --> 00:40:28,250
I like that in a man.

689
00:40:28,876 --> 00:40:30,878
Well, I'm glad.

690
00:40:31,629 --> 00:40:34,089
I'm a lousy liar.
So you're right.

691
00:40:34,924 --> 00:40:37,593
Actually, there's, uh, there's
something I wanted to tell you.

692
00:40:38,677 --> 00:40:39,720
What?

693
00:40:40,846 --> 00:40:44,141
That work stuff...
it's actually a promotion.

694
00:40:44,266 --> 00:40:45,476
Really? What kind?

695
00:40:45,601 --> 00:40:49,313
The bureau is forming a task force
to deal with international art theft.

696
00:40:49,438 --> 00:40:51,649
It'll be working with Scotland yard
and other agencies.

697
00:40:51,774 --> 00:40:52,983
Long story short...

698
00:40:53,734 --> 00:40:56,195
they, uh... they... they...
they want me to run it.

699
00:40:56,320 --> 00:40:58,489
Congratulations. That's fantastic.

700
00:40:58,614 --> 00:41:00,658
Yeah, but they want me
to run it from D.C.

701
00:41:05,579 --> 00:41:08,457
- So I'm telling them "no."
- You can't do that.

702
00:41:09,458 --> 00:41:11,669
- I'm allowed.
- It's a great job, okay?

703
00:41:11,794 --> 00:41:13,128
You can't turn it down.

704
00:41:13,671 --> 00:41:14,880
I'm not a kid.

705
00:41:15,923 --> 00:41:17,967
I've been married and divorced.

706
00:41:21,136 --> 00:41:22,471
I know when something's real.

707
00:41:23,973 --> 00:41:25,724
And when it could get serious.

708
00:41:28,394 --> 00:41:29,895
I feel that way about us.

709
00:41:32,439 --> 00:41:34,692
Do you... Feel that way?

710
00:41:39,905 --> 00:41:41,031
Yeah, I do.

711
00:41:43,409 --> 00:41:46,495
Look, one thing I know is that when
you feel that way about someone

712
00:41:46,662 --> 00:41:52,167
you got to hang on, 'cause...
because it doesn't happen very often.

713
00:41:53,585 --> 00:41:54,837
Marcus.

714
00:41:55,504 --> 00:41:59,258
You cannot turn down this job.
They are not gonna offer it to you again.

715
00:41:59,383 --> 00:42:00,509
I know.

716
00:42:01,552 --> 00:42:03,804
- It's okay.
- No, it's not.

717
00:42:07,016 --> 00:42:10,102
At the same time, the truth is,
I don't want you to go.

718
00:42:11,020 --> 00:42:12,187
I really don't.

719
00:42:14,064 --> 00:42:15,482
What if you came with me?

720
00:42:18,027 --> 00:42:19,153
What?

721
00:42:23,574 --> 00:42:25,325
I'm asking what if...

722
00:42:26,910 --> 00:42:29,788
We went to D.C...
You and me, together?

723
00:42:32,207 --> 00:42:33,876
What would you think
of that, Teresa?


